<node id="paper:W2997631644">
  <data key="d0">Paper</data>
  <data key="d1">ReducedSqNet: A Shallow Architecture for CIFAR-10</data>
  <data key="d6">ReducedSqNet: A Shallow Architecture for CIFAR-10</data>
  <data key="d5">2018</data>
  <data key="d7">https://doi.org/10.1109/csci46756.2018.00078</data>
  <data key="d8">5</data>
  <data key="d9">The field of computer vision and its sub discipline: classification, object detection and semantic segmentation have seen tremendous growth since the introduction of convolution neural network. Despite of these success, the deployment of convolution neural networks on an embedded device or micro architecture system has been a matter of concern as convolution neural networks are memory and computationally expensive. Several advanced technique used to address this issues are: pruning based on ranks of filters, use of compact convolution filters, quantization, sharing and encoding filter weights. This paper focuses on using compact convolution filter to reduce the number of parameters in the model. The architecture proposed in the paper is a shallow counter part of the SqueezeNet architecture</data>
</node>
<node id="author:durvesh pathak">
  <data key="d0">Author</data>
  <data key="d1">Durvesh Pathak</data>
</node>
<node id="paper:W2950843262">
  <data key="d0">Paper</data>
  <data key="d1">CIFAR-10: KNN-based Ensemble of Classifiers</data>
  <data key="d6">CIFAR-10: KNN-based Ensemble of Classifiers</data>
  <data key="d5">2016</data>
  <data key="d7">https://doi.org/10.48550/arxiv.1611.04905</data>
  <data key="d8">7</data>
  <data key="d9">In this paper, we study the performance of different classifiers on the CIFAR-10 dataset, and build an ensemble of classifiers to reach a better performance. We show that, on CIFAR-10, K-Nearest Neighbors (KNN) and Convolutional Neural Network (CNN), on some classes, are mutually exclusive, thus yield in higher accuracy when combined. We reduce KNN overfitting using Principal Component Analysis (PCA), and ensemble it with a CNN to increase its accuracy. Our approach improves our best CNN model from 93.33% to 94.03%.</data>
</node>
<node id="paper:W4287028333">
  <data key="d0">Paper</data>
  <data key="d1">Improved deterministic l2 robustness on CIFAR-10 and CIFAR-100</data>
  <data key="d6">Improved deterministic l2 robustness on CIFAR-10 and CIFAR-100</data>
  <data key="d5">2021</data>
  <data key="d7">https://doi.org/10.48550/arxiv.2108.04062</data>
  <data key="d8">10</data>
  <data key="d9">Training convolutional neural networks (CNNs) with a strict Lipschitz constraint under the $l_{2}$ norm is useful for provable adversarial robustness, interpretable gradients and stable training. While $1$-Lipschitz CNNs can be designed by enforcing a $1$-Lipschitz constraint on each layer, training such networks requires each layer to have an orthogonal Jacobian matrix (for all inputs) to prevent the gradients from vanishing during backpropagation. A layer with this property is said to be Gradient Norm Preserving (GNP). In this work, we introduce a procedure to certify the robustness of $1$-Lipschitz CNNs by relaxing the orthogonalization of the last linear layer of the network that significantly advances the state of the art for both standard and provable robust accuracies on CIFAR-100 (</data>
</node>
