<node id="paper:W4403126298">
  <data key="d0">Paper</data>
  <data key="d1">Achieving Near-Perfect Accuracy in CIFAR-10 Classification</data>
  <data key="d6">Achieving Near-Perfect Accuracy in CIFAR-10 Classification</data>
  <data key="d5">2024</data>
  <data key="d7">https://doi.org/10.1109/icait61638.2024.10690610</data>
  <data key="d8">5</data>
  <data key="d9">We inaugurate our study on performing a near-perfect accuracy of 99.95% in the classification of the CIFAR-10 dataset using a CNN based model which were trained from scratch. The CIFAR-l0 dataset is a distinguished benchmark for image classification, containing of 10 classes such as airplanes, cars, cats, birds, and more. While more than a few researchers often depend on pretrained models or transfer learning techniques to accomplish great accuracy on this dataset, we manifest that outstanding performance can be achieved without utilizing any pretrained weights. Our methods necessitate designing a CNN architecture with numerous convolutional layers, batch normalization, and fully connected layers, blended with a fine-tuned optimization strategy. This model is trained over a prolonged numbe</data>
</node>
