<node id="paper:W1825672851">
  <data key="d0">Paper</data>
  <data key="d1">Training deep neural networks with low precision multiplications</data>
  <data key="d6">Training deep neural networks with low precision multiplications</data>
  <data key="d5">2014</data>
  <data key="d7">https://doi.org/10.48550/arxiv.1412.7024</data>
  <data key="d8">407</data>
  <data key="d9">Multipliers are the most space and power-hungry arithmetic operators of the digital implementation of deep neural networks. We train a set of state-of-the-art neural networks (Maxout networks) on three benchmark datasets: MNIST, CIFAR-10 and SVHN. They are trained with three distinct formats: floating point, fixed point and dynamic fixed point. For each of those datasets and for each of those formats, we assess the impact of the precision of the multiplications on the final error after training. We find that very low precision is sufficient not just for running trained networks but also for training them. For example, it is possible to train Maxout networks with 10 bits multiplications.</data>
</node>
<node id="author:matthieu courbariaux">
  <data key="d0">Author</data>
  <data key="d1">Matthieu Courbariaux</data>
