<node id="paper:W7135217380">
  <data key="d0">Paper</data>
  <data key="d1">A comparative study of federated learning algorithms on Tiny ImageNet</data>
  <data key="d6">A comparative study of federated learning algorithms on Tiny ImageNet</data>
  <data key="d5">2026</data>
  <data key="d7">https://doi.org/10.1117/12.3108397</data>
  <data key="d8">0</data>
  <data key="d9">Federated Learning (FL) has become a promising framework for collaborative model training across distributed clients without sharing raw data, addressing privacy concerns in sensitive fields. However, performance often deteriorates when client data are non-independent and identically distributed (non-IID), leading to unstable convergence and accuracy degradation. This study assesses the effectiveness of three representative algorithms of federated learning – Federated Average (FedAvg), Federated Proximal (FedPro) and Federated Adaptive Optimization (FedOpt) on the Tiny ImageNet dataset using convolutional neural networks including Resnet-18, MobileNetV2, and ResNet-50. The experiments focus on assessing algorithm stability, convergence rate, and accuracy under both IID and non-IID data par</data>
</node>
<node id="author:junqi wang">
  <data key="d0">Author</data>
  <data key="d1">Junqi Wang</data>
</node>
<node id="paper:W3133106089">
  <data key="d0">Paper</data>
  <data key="d1">Resizing Tiny Imagenet: An Iterative Approach Towards Image Classification</data>
  <data key="d6">Resizing Tiny Imagenet: An Iterative Approach Towards Image Classification</data>
  <data key="d5">2020</data>
  <data key="d7">https://doi.org/10.21276/ijircst.2020.8.6.8</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
  <data key="d9">Deep neural networks have attained almost human-level performance over several Image classification and object detection problems, deploying robust and powerful state-of-the-art networks.Stanford's Tiny ImageNet dataset has been around for a while and neural networks have struggled to classify them.It is generally considered one of the harder datasets in the domain of image classification.The validation accuracy of the existing systems maxes out at 61-62% with a select few shooting beyond 68-69%.These approaches are often plagued by problems of overfitting and vanishing gradients.In this paper, we present a new method to get above average validation accuracy while circumventing these problems.We use the resizing image technique which trains multiple model times over different image sizes.T</data>
</node>
