<node id="paper:W7203938179">
  <data key="d0">Paper</data>
  <data key="d1">Concrete crack prediction using a fine-tuned model trained on tiny-imagenet</data>
  <data key="d6">Concrete crack prediction using a fine-tuned model trained on tiny-imagenet</data>
  <data key="d5">2026</data>
  <data key="d7">https://doi.org/10.1201/9781003778677-180</data>
  <data key="d8">0</data>
  <data key="d9">Loading tests were conducted on two series of RC specimens, designated S and F, and vibration measurement data were employed to detect crack position. For this purpose, we utilized a trained convolutional neural network model on Tiny-ImageNet, a subset of the ImageNet Large Scale Visual Recognition Challenge (ILSVRC). The results indicate that this model can predict crack patterns effectively even when only a small portion of its layers is fine-tuned. This suggests that low-level image features and general pattern recognition capabilities acquired from Tiny-ImageNet can be successfully transferred to crack detection tasks. Consequently, the proposed approach shortens training time, reduces computational burden, and facilitates efficient and scalable damage detection in RC structures.</data>
</node>
<node id="author:h. yasukawa">
  <data key="d0">Author</data>
