  <data key="d0">Paper</data>
  <data key="d1">Automated Image Colorization Using Deep Learning</data>
  <data key="d6">Automated Image Colorization Using Deep Learning</data>
  <data key="d5">2025</data>
  <data key="d7">https://doi.org/10.47392/irjaem.2025.0446</data>
  <data key="d8">0</data>
  <data key="d9">Restoring colors to grayscale images has always been a complex challenge in computer vision because a single shade of gray could represent multiple possible colors. Early rule-based and manual methods often gave lifeless or unrealistic outputs. With the rise of deep learning, however, major improvements have been made using CNNs, transfer learning, and generative models. In this work, we present an Efficient Net-based model for automatic image colorization, trained on the large-scale Places365 dataset. To enhance the richness of colors, we introduce a color rebalancing technique that gives more weight to rarely used hues. While traditional evaluation metrics like Mean Squared Error (MSE) remain common, they fail to capture how humans actually perceive image quality. Our results show that t</data>
</node>
