<node id="paper:W4416341582">
  <data key="d0">Paper</data>
  <data key="d1">Fine-tuning Deep Convolutional Neural Networks on Caltech-101: An Application of Transfer Learning</data>
  <data key="d6">Fine-tuning Deep Convolutional Neural Networks on Caltech-101: An Application of Transfer Learning</data>
  <data key="d5">2025</data>
  <data key="d7">https://doi.org/10.1109/naecon65708.2025.11235477</data>
  <data key="d8">0</data>
  <data key="d9">Transfer learning is a time-efficient technique, which leverages a pre-trained model and applies it to a different, but similar dataset. This study uses transfer learning to drive the creation of a high-performing fine-tuned convolutional neural network (CNN) for the Caltech-101 dataset in a set of four experiments. A broad assessment of eighteen pre-trained CNNs was initially considered in the first experiment, with three follow-on experiments for model down-selection and hyperparameter fine-tuning. The CNNs and hyperparameter values were based on a combination of classification accuracy, model runtime, and loss. The best-performing model was based on the MobileNet architecture which yielded a 93.4% accuracy after training for less than one minute. The hyperparameters with the greatest im</data>
</node>
