<node id="paper:W7222954732">
  <data key="d0">Paper</data>
  <data key="d1">Supervised Classification via Energy-Based Learning on CIFAR10 CIFAR100 and CELEBA</data>
  <data key="d6">Supervised Classification via Energy-Based Learning on CIFAR10 CIFAR100 and CELEBA</data>
  <data key="d5">2026</data>
  <data key="d7">https://doi.org/10.15408/jti.v19i2.48655</data>
  <data key="d8">0</data>
  <data key="d9">This research evaluates a Joint Energy Model (JEM) approach to perform supervised classification on the CIFAR10, CIFAR100, and CELEBA datasets. We implement a range of architectures, including custom shallow and deep CNNs and an adapted ResNet-18 modified with 3x3 first-layer convolution to prevent the loss of critical spatial information for small image classification. Following a rigorous experimental protocol involving multiple independent runs, we provide statistical validation via mean and standard deviation for all performance metrics. Our models achieved F1-scores exceeding 86% for both CIFAR datasets and a 90.34% accuracy of CELEBA multi-label classification. Beyond accuracy, we conducted a systematic analysis of the learned energy landscapes. While results confirm a systemic calib</data>
</node>
