<node id="paper:W7165511665">
  <data key="d0">Paper</data>
  <data key="d1">Car classification</data>
  <data key="d6">Car classification</data>
  <data key="d5">2026</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8">0</data>
  <data key="d9">This bachelor's thesis deals with the design and implementation of a system for automatic vehicle detection and classification based on images from an overview camera with an oblique side view. The proposed solution is implemented as a two-stage pipeline combining a vehicle detector with a brand classifier. Four detection models were trained on the MIO-TCD and UA-DETRAC datasets and two classification models on the Stanford Cars and VMMRdb datasets. A dataset of 2004 images from CAMEA cameras was manually annotated and both selected models were fine-tuned on this data. The resulting detector achieved mAP50 = 95.9% on the test set and the brand classifier achieved top-1 = 80.5%. The pipeline was implemented in Python and wrapped into a DLL library enabling integration into external systems.</data>
</node>
<node id="author:michal fidrich">
  <data key="d0">Author</data>
  <data key="d1">Michal Fidrich</data>
</node>
<node id="paper:W3108752471">
  <data key="d0">Paper</data>
  <data key="d1">Neural Prototype Trees for Interpretable Fine-grained Image Recognition</data>
  <data key="d6">Neural Prototype Trees for Interpretable Fine-grained Image Recognition</data>
  <data key="d5">2021</data>
  <data key="d7">https://doi.org/10.1109/cvpr46437.2021.01469</data>
  <data key="d8">268</data>
  <data key="d9">Prototype-based methods use interpretable representations to address the black-box nature of deep learning models, in contrast to post-hoc explanation methods that only approximate such models. We propose the Neural Prototype Tree (ProtoTree), an intrinsically interpretable deep learning method for fine-grained image recognition. ProtoTree combines prototype learning with decision trees, and thus results in a globally interpretable model by design. Additionally, ProtoTree can locally explain a single prediction by outlining a decision path through the tree. Each node in our binary tree contains a trainable prototypical part. The presence or absence of this learned prototype in an image determines the routing through a node. Decision making is therefore similar to human reasoning: Does the </data>
</node>
