<node id="paper:W4244868965">
  <data key="d0">Paper</data>
  <data key="d1">The cityscape</data>
  <data key="d6">The cityscape</data>
  <data key="d5">2016</data>
  <data key="d7">https://doi.org/10.1332/policypress/9781447322917.003.0004</data>
  <data key="d8">0</data>
  <data key="d9">This chapter centers on the actual physical-natural substances of African urban environments, but also on the imaginary – the symbolic and spiritual conceptualizations of those landscapes, as seen for instance in place-names - with Zanzibar as the featured city. Conceptually, the chapter builds from both African studies scholarship and from what was once called the ‘new’ cultural geography. It examines the cityscape physically, politically and metaphysically, arguing for the significance of spiritual cityscapes to everyday cultural understandings of urban environments as well as the generally common pattern of risk in terms of physical settings (emphasizing vulnerabilities to climate change). Emphasis on the importance of religion and spirituality in African cityscapes is not about further</data>
</node>
<node id="paper:W4243930181">
  <data key="d0">Paper</data>
  <data key="d1">Cityscapes</data>
  <data key="d6">Cityscapes</data>
  <data key="d5">2014</data>
  <data key="d7">https://doi.org/10.4324/9780203108703-4</data>
  <data key="d8">0</data>
  <data key="d9">Urban places vary greatly in their cityscape: this is the case both within a particular city and through comparison with other cities. This diversity is created by two groups or sets of drivers: the actions and behaviour of all the living creatures, especially people, and the impact of biophysical phenomena, including violent geophysical events such as storms, high winds, landslides, tsunamis, earthquakes and volcanic eruptions. This resultant variety is a reflection of a complex interaction of physical, natural and social factors which provide not only a place for people to live and work, but also the habitats required for wildlife and plants. Wildlife is opportunistic and adaptable and finds and adjusts to habitat niches on, in and around buildings. Human actions may be beneficial or dam</data>
</node>
