<node id="paper:W2066186317">
  <data key="d0">Paper</data>
  <data key="d1">Dick Tracy’s Cityscape</data>
  <data key="d6">Dick Tracy’s Cityscape</data>
  <data key="d5">1993</data>
  <data key="d7">https://doi.org/10.1353/pcg.1993.0013</data>
  <data key="d8">13</data>
  <data key="d9">Landscape portrayal in film and in comics influences our understanding and perception of the world. The cityscape of the movie Dick Tracy (1990) is Warren Beatty’s interpretation of the cityscape portrayed in Chester Gould’s (1930) comic strip of the same name. Each rendition offers clues to the social milieu in which they were created. Both cityscapes are generic, reflect anti-urban sentiment, and offer a simplistic, moralistic view of reality.</data>
</node>
