<node id="paper:W4206340529">
  <data key="d0">Paper</data>
  <data key="d1">Forming Cityscapes</data>
  <data key="d6">Forming Cityscapes</data>
  <data key="d5">2021</data>
  <data key="d7">https://doi.org/10.5117/9789463729505_ch05</data>
  <data key="d8">0</data>
  <data key="d9">This chapter presents a photographic documentation project with a particular interest in everyday city life in Singapore. As a theoretical reflection on the project, we examine the interrelationships between the project and related ideas across urban studies, photography and design, while positioning it as a form of ‘artistic research’. Selected photographic findings from Forming Cityscapes are presented alongside a critical discussion on creative forms of appropriation that indirectly critique ‘top-down’ design implementations and suggest other micro-possibilities through actual use. With this, an imaginative representation of Singapore’s cityscape is represented through (1) our creative practice, and (2) photographic findings of creative practices found in the city.</data>
</node>
