<node id="paper:W3198161249">
  <data key="d0">Paper</data>
  <data key="d1">Recalibrating the KITTI Dataset Camera Setup for Improved Odometry Accuracy</data>
  <data key="d6">Recalibrating the KITTI Dataset Camera Setup for Improved Odometry Accuracy</data>
  <data key="d5">2021</data>
  <data key="d7">https://doi.org/10.1109/ecmr50962.2021.9568821</data>
  <data key="d8">24</data>
  <data key="d9">Over the last decade, one of the most relevant public datasets for evaluating odometry accuracy is the KITTI dataset. Beside the quality and rich sensor setup, its success is also due to the online evaluation tool, which enables researchers to bench-mark and compare algorithms. The results are evaluated on the test subset solely, without any knowledge about the ground truth, yielding unbiased, overfit free and therefore relevant validation for robot localization based on cameras, 3D laser or combination of both. However, as any sensor setup, it requires prior calibration and rectified stereo images are provided, introducing dependence on the default calibration parameters. Given that, a natural question arises if a better set of calibration parameters can be found that would yield higher o</data>
</node>
