<node id="paper:W7117466429">
  <data key="d0">Paper</data>
  <data key="d1">A Cross-Paradigm Evaluation of SLAM Algorithms for Autonomous Driving on the KITTI Dataset</data>
  <data key="d6">A Cross-Paradigm Evaluation of SLAM Algorithms for Autonomous Driving on the KITTI Dataset</data>
  <data key="d5">2025</data>
  <data key="d7">https://doi.org/10.1109/cinti67731.2025.11311809</data>
  <data key="d8">0</data>
  <data key="d9">Simultaneous Localization and Mapping (SLAM) is a cornerstone technology for autonomous systems, particularly in autonomous driving. Although numerous studies benchmark SLAM algorithms, a significant gap exists in the literature regarding direct, cross-paradigm comparisons, evaluating systems based on disparate sensor modalities under identical conditions on public datasets. This paper addresses this gap by presenting a comprehensive qualitative and quantitative evaluation of several state-of-the-art SLAM algorithms. The EKF, MSCKF, OKVIS, ORB-SLAM3, F-LOAM and LVI-SAM algorithms are qualitatively compared, but only the last four algorithms were evaluated using the publicly available KITTI (Karlsruhe Institute of Technology and Toyota Technological Institute) dataset. The findings indicate</data>
</node>
<node id="author:péter rózsás">
  <data key="d0">Author</data>
  <data key="d1">Péter Rózsás</data>
</node>
