<node id="paper:W4416183728">
  <data key="d0">Paper</data>
  <data key="d1">Risk-Based Filtering of Valuable Driving Situations in the Waymo Open Motion Dataset</data>
  <data key="d6">Risk-Based Filtering of Valuable Driving Situations in the Waymo Open Motion Dataset</data>
  <data key="d5">2025</data>
  <data key="d7">https://doi.org/10.1109/iavvc61942.2025.11219578</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
  <data key="d9">Improving automated vehicle software requires driving data rich in valuable road user interactions. In this paper, we propose a risk-based filtering approach that helps identify such valuable driving situations from large datasets. Specifically, we use a probabilistic risk model to detect high-risk situations. Our method stands out by considering a) first-order situations (where one vehicle directly influences another and induces risk) and b) second-order situations (where influence propagates through an intermediary vehicle). In experiments, we show that our approach effectively selects valuable driving situations in the Waymo Open Motion Dataset. Compared to the two baseline interaction metrics of Kalman difficulty and Tracks-To-Predict (TTP), our filtering approach identifies complex an</data>
</node>
<node id="author:tim puphal">
  <data key="d0">Author</data>
  <data key="d1">Tim Puphal</data>
</node>
<node id="author:vipul ramtekkar">
