<node id="paper:W2141320374">
  <data key="d0">Paper</data>
  <data key="d1">UCF-101 mitigates streptozotocin-induced cardiomyocyte dysfunction: role of AMPK</data>
  <data key="d6">UCF-101 mitigates streptozotocin-induced cardiomyocyte dysfunction: role of AMPK</data>
  <data key="d5">2009</data>
  <data key="d7">https://doi.org/10.1152/ajpendo.00323.2009</data>
  <data key="d8">29</data>
  <data key="d9">Diabetic heart disease contributes to the high mortality in diabetics, although effective clinical management is lacking. The protease inhibitor 5-[5-(2-nitrophenyl) furfuryliodine]-1,3-diphenyl-2-thiobarbituric acid (UCF-101) was reported to protect the hearts against ischemic injury. This study examined the role of UCF-101 on streptozotocin (STZ)-induced diabetic heart defect. Vehicle or UCF-101 was administrated to STZ diabetic mice, and cardiomyocyte mechanical properties were analyzed. UCF-101 reduced STZ-induced hyperglycemia and alleviated STZ-induced aberration in cardiomyocyte contractile mechanics. Diabetes dramatically decreased AMPK phosphorylation at Thr(172) of catalytic alpha-subunit, which was restored by UCF-101. Neither diabetes nor UCF-101 affected the expression of HtrA</data>
</node>
