  <data key="d8">0</data>
  <data key="d9">Joint-Embedding Predictive Architectures (JEPA) are a promising framework for self-supervised video representation learning, yet the behavior of auxiliary objectives in small-scale Video-JEPA training is not well characterized. We report a small-scale empirical study of 18 auxiliary objective variants for Video-JEPA across two pretraining regimes: single-dataset (UCF-101) and mixed-dataset (UCF-101 + Something-Something V2 + ImageNet-100). We evaluate frozen representations on three complementary benchmarks: Diving-48 (fine-grained motion), SomethingSomething V2 (temporal reasoning), and Image Research goal: How does the computational throughput of Video-JEPA models with different auxiliary objective variants scale with increasing input frame resolution during fine-tuning on UCF-101? Auton</data>
</node>
