<node id="author:aaron c. courville">
  <data key="d0">Author</data>
  <data key="d1">Aaron C. Courville</data>
</node>
<node id="paper:W2907143950">
  <data key="d0">Paper</data>
  <data key="d1">CLEVR-Ref+: Diagnosing Visual Reasoning With Referring Expressions</data>
  <data key="d6">CLEVR-Ref+: Diagnosing Visual Reasoning With Referring Expressions</data>
  <data key="d5">2019</data>
  <data key="d7">https://doi.org/10.1109/cvpr.2019.00431</data>
  <data key="d8">111</data>
  <data key="d9">Referring object detection and referring image segmentation are important tasks that require joint understanding of visual information and natural language. Yet there has been evidence that current benchmark datasets suffer from bias, and current state-of-the-art models cannot be easily evaluated on their intermediate reasoning process. To address these issues and complement similar efforts in visual question answering, we build CLEVR-Ref+, a synthetic diagnostic dataset for referring expression comprehension. The precise locations and attributes of the objects are readily available, and the referring expressions are automatically associated with functional programs. The synthetic nature allows control over dataset bias (through sampling strategy), and the modular programs enable intermedi</data>
</node>
<node id="author:runtao liu">
  <data key="d0">Author</data>
  <data key="d1">Runtao Liu</data>
</node>
<node id="author:yutong bai">
  <data key="d0">Author</data>
  <data key="d1">Yutong Bai</data>
</node>
<node id="paper:W2950297087">
  <data key="d0">Paper</data>
  <data key="d1">CLEVR-Dialog: A Diagnostic Dataset for Multi-Round Reasoning in Visual Dialog</data>
  <data key="d6">CLEVR-Dialog: A Diagnostic Dataset for Multi-Round Reasoning in Visual Dialog</data>
  <data key="d5">2019</data>
  <data key="d7">https://doi.org/10.48550/arxiv.1903.03166</data>
  <data key="d8">51</data>
  <data key="d9">Visual Dialog is a multimodal task of answering a sequence of questions grounded in an image, using the conversation history as context. It entails challenges in vision, language, reasoning, and grounding. However, studying these subtasks in isolation on large, real datasets is infeasible as it requires prohibitively-expensive complete annotation of the 'state' of all images and dialogs. We develop CLEVR-Dialog, a large diagnostic dataset for studying multi-round reasoning in visual dialog. Specifically, we construct a dialog grammar that is grounded in the scene graphs of the images from the CLEVR dataset. This combination results in a dataset where all aspects of the visual dialog are fully annotated. In total, CLEVR-Dialog contains 5 instances of 10-round dialogs for about 85k CLEVR ima</data>
</node>
<node id="author:satwik kottur">
  <data key="d0">Author</data>
  <data key="d1">Satwik Kottur</data>
</node>
<node id="author:josé m. f. moura">
  <data key="d0">Author</data>
  <data key="d1">José M. F. Moura</data>
</node>
<node id="author:devi parikh">
  <data key="d0">Author</data>
  <data key="d1">Devi Parikh</data>
</node>
<node id="author:dhruv batra">
  <data key="d0">Author</data>
  <data key="d1">Dhruv Batra</data>
</node>
<node id="author:marcus rohrbach">
  <data key="d0">Author</data>
  <data key="d1">Marcus Rohrbach</data>
</node>
<node id="paper:W2561715562">
  <data key="d0">Paper</data>
  <data key="d1">CLEVR: A Diagnostic Dataset for Compositional Language and Elementary Visual Reasoning</data>
  <data key="d6">CLEVR: A Diagnostic Dataset for Compositional Language and Elementary Visual Reasoning</data>
  <data key="d5">2017</data>
  <data key="d7">https://doi.org/10.1109/cvpr.2017.215</data>
  <data key="d8">1780</data>
  <data key="d9">When building artificial intelligence systems that can reason and answer questions about visual data, we need diagnostic tests to analyze our progress and discover short-comings. Existing benchmarks for visual question answering can help, but have strong biases that models can exploit to correctly answer questions without reasoning. They also conflate multiple sources of error, making it hard to pinpoint model weaknesses. We present a diagnostic dataset that tests a range of visual reasoning abilities. It contains minimal biases and has detailed annotations describing the kind of reasoning each question requires. We use this dataset to analyze a variety of modern visual reasoning systems, providing novel insights into their abilities and limitations.</data>
</node>
<node id="author:justin johnson">
  <data key="d0">Author</data>
  <data key="d1">Justin Johnson</data>
