<node id="paper:W4410339115">
  <data key="d0">Paper</data>
  <data key="d1">Supervised Learning and SCST Image Captioning on Flickr30k: Analyzing Metric-Driven Trade-offs in Semantic Performance</data>
  <data key="d6">Supervised Learning and SCST Image Captioning on Flickr30k: Analyzing Metric-Driven Trade-offs in Semantic Performance</data>
  <data key="d5">2025</data>
  <data key="d7">https://doi.org/10.55248/gengpi.6.0425.1697</data>
  <data key="d8">0</data>
  <data key="d9">Image captioning plays a pivotal role in enhancing accessibility, searchability, and human-computer interaction by automatically generating natural language descriptions for images.While standard encoder-decoder models trained using Supervised Learning (SL) with cross-entropy loss optimize word-level accuracy, they often fail to align with sequence-level evaluation metrics such as BLEU, CIDEr, and SPICE.Additionally, these models suffer from exposure bias.This paper explores augmenting SL with Reinforcement Learning (RL), specifically Self-Critical Sequence Training (SCST), to optimize the CIDEr metric, which better correlates with human consensus judgments.Using the Flickr30k dataset, we employed an attention-based ResNet-101 encoder and an LSTM decoder.In the SL phase, the model achieved</data>
