<node id="paper:W3102925419">
  <data key="d0">Paper</data>
  <data key="d1">Event Extraction by Answering (Almost) Natural Questions</data>
  <data key="d6">Event Extraction by Answering (Almost) Natural Questions</data>
  <data key="d5">2020</data>
  <data key="d7">https://doi.org/10.18653/v1/2020.emnlp-main.49</data>
  <data key="d8">428</data>
  <data key="d9">The problem of event extraction requires detecting the event trigger and extracting its corresponding arguments.Existing work in event argument extraction typically relies heavily on entity recognition as a preprocessing/concurrent step, causing the well-known problem of error propagation.To avoid this issue, we introduce a new paradigm for event extraction by formulating it as a question answering (QA) task that extracts the event arguments in an end-to-end manner.Empirical results demonstrate that our framework outperforms prior methods substantially; in addition, it is capable of extracting event arguments for roles not seen at training time (i.e., in a zeroshot learning setting).1</data>
</node>
