<node id="paper:W4404700149">
  <data key="d0">Paper</data>
  <data key="d1">Seneca’s Natural Questions</data>
  <data key="d6">Seneca’s Natural Questions</data>
  <data key="d5">2024</data>
  <data key="d7">https://doi.org/10.1093/9780197787588.003.0004</data>
  <data key="d8">0</data>
  <data key="d9">Abstract Seneca’s Natural Questions configures meteorology as a turbulent adventure that potentially strengthens the mind, ennobles the spirit, and reveals the greatness of the cosmos, which is identical with god; it is an experience of the sublime. Amidst the theatrical duplicity of Nero’s Rome, this journey is full of false starts, and Phaethon remains an important model. Since certain knowledge is often elusive, Seneca embraces aporia and dares to think the unthinkable—perhaps comets are planets with unfathomable orbits. Similarly, Stoicism’s promise of moral progress can appear deceptive—we might briefly attain the sage’s serenity, but the best outcome for which the proficiens can hope is a paradoxical state of vital calmness. Worse still, competing sources of sublimity, including unre</data>
</node>
