<node id="paper:W7214594854">
  <data key="d0">Paper</data>
  <data key="d1">Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning of BERT: A Study of Fixed-Rank and Layer-Wise LoRA on SST-2</data>
  <data key="d6">Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning of BERT: A Study of Fixed-Rank and Layer-Wise LoRA on SST-2</data>
  <data key="d5">2026</data>
  <data key="d7">https://doi.org/10.54254/2755-2721/2026.37399</data>
  <data key="d8">0</data>
  <data key="d9">Parameter-efficient fine-tuning (PEFT) has emerged as a crucial method for adapting pretrained Transformer models to downstream tasks without the need to fine-tune the full set of model parameters. Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA) is a technique that reduces the number of trainable parameters by representing weight updates with low-rank matrices. This paper explores the impact of LoRA rank selection and layer-wise rank allocation on the trade-off between classification accuracy and the number of parameters to be fine-tuned through fine-tuning BERT-base-uncased on the sentiment classification task, SST-2. The three fixed-rank LoRA settings with ranks of 4, 8, and 16 are first investigated, as well as a manually-designed layer-wise LoRA setting that assigns different ranks to different subsets of </data>
</node>
