<node id="paper:W4411484612">
  <data key="d0">Paper</data>
  <data key="d1">Exploring Movie Sentiments: A Comparative Analysis of Deep Learning, Machine Learning, and Bayesian Approaches on IMDb Reviews</data>
  <data key="d6">Exploring Movie Sentiments: A Comparative Analysis of Deep Learning, Machine Learning, and Bayesian Approaches on IMDb Reviews</data>
  <data key="d5">2025</data>
  <data key="d7">https://doi.org/10.1109/icdsbs63635.2025.11031975</data>
  <data key="d8">0</data>
  <data key="d9">Sentiment analysis serves a significant function in those large volumes of text data, like product reviews or company feedback, make organizations better informed. Deep learning models, such as Long Short-Term Memory Networks (LSTM), are very proficient in modeling sequential information and, therefore, very effective with regard to sentiment classification tasks like IMDb reviews. Traditional machine learning methods like Multinomial Naive Bayes (MNB) and Logistic Regression (LR) work almost as well. MNB combined with feature extraction like TF-IDF and feature selection techniques such as Information Gain yields nearly 90.59score. LR is simple and reliable and works well on huge text datasets. Using a Voting Assembler, combining predictions from multiple models, leads to improving precisi</data>
</node>
<node id="author:venkata datta sai krishna kona">
  <data key="d0">Author</data>
