  <data key="d1">Luís Fred</data>
</node>
<node id="paper:W2747273402">
  <data key="d0">Paper</data>
  <data key="d1">Reactions to "Ag News" Releases: Survey of Idaho Newspaper Editors and Broadcast News Directors</data>
  <data key="d6">Reactions to "Ag News" Releases: Survey of Idaho Newspaper Editors and Broadcast News Directors</data>
  <data key="d5">1987</data>
  <data key="d7">https://doi.org/10.4148/1051-0834.1594</data>
  <data key="d8">3</data>
  <data key="d9">Communications specialists at the University of Idaho College of Agriculture (UI) surveyed the Idaho media in October 1986 to determine evaluations of "Ag News" releases. Of 166 surveys mailed, 104 were returned (63 percent). Results indicated that the UI news releases were well accepted and rated, at least, "very good." A majority of respondents said the releases were "generally understandable to the public," that the maximum length of a story usually reprinted in full was two pages; and that the news releases were preferable to "tip" sheets for all media except television.</data>
</node>
<node id="author:marlene a. fritz">
  <data key="d0">Author</data>
  <data key="d1">Marlene A. Fritz</data>
</node>
<node id="paper:W4415418581">
  <data key="d0">Paper</data>
  <data key="d1">Enhancing AG News Classification With Hypergraph Attention Networks and Quadratic SVM</data>
  <data key="d6">Enhancing AG News Classification With Hypergraph Attention Networks and Quadratic SVM</data>
  <data key="d5">2025</data>
  <data key="d7">https://doi.org/10.1111/exsy.70150</data>
  <data key="d8">2</data>
  <data key="d9">ABSTRACT News text classification is a technique of classifying news articles into some predefined classes. It helps consumers find news that piques their interest. Due to the growth of internet news content, effective automatic classification systems are required to handle and arrange massive volumes of news articles. Here, the AG's News Corpus (AG News) articles are classified through the hypergraph neural network along with the attention layer and quadratic support vector machine (AGNews_HAL_QSVM). This benchmark dataset was named after the ‘ComeToMyHead’ project by Alberto G. (AG) Leonardo. The dataset was gathered from Kaggle, and the LDA (Latent Dirichlet Allocation) was used to generate the topic‐specific data. Every topic will be regarded as a hyperedge in the hypergraph, and each </data>
</node>
