  <data key="d1">Hassan AABIDI</data>
</node>
<node id="paper:W7220595469">
  <data key="d0">Paper</data>
  <data key="d1">Comparative Evaluation of Text Similarity Matrices for Enhanced Abstractive Summarization on CNN/Dailymail Corpus</data>
  <data key="d6">Comparative Evaluation of Text Similarity Matrices for Enhanced Abstractive Summarization on CNN/Dailymail Corpus</data>
  <data key="d5">2023</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8">0</data>
  <data key="d9">The importance of extracting essential information from extensive textual data is becoming increasingly crucial in our contemporary data-abundant environment. Using the CNN/Dailymail dataset, this study rigorously investigates the effect of text similarity matrices—including Cosine Similarity, Jaccard Index, and the Universal Sentence Encoder (USE)—on abstractive text summarization. It assesses how these matrices influence the quality of summaries. The research utilises comprehensive assessment techniques, such as BLEU scores, to examine the performance of each matrix. This delineates the effectiveness and limitations of employing quantitative analysis and graphical representations to succinctly and coherently summarize extensive textual content. While the Cosine Similarity metric exhibits</data>
</node>
<node id="author:muhammad hammad asif">
  <data key="d0">Author</data>
  <data key="d1">Muhammad Hammad Asif</data>
</node>
<node id="author:aman ullah yaseen">
  <data key="d0">Author</data>
  <data key="d1">Aman Ullah Yaseen</data>
</node>
<node id="paper:W7165791233">
  <data key="d0">Paper</data>
  <data key="d1">TRANSFORMER-BASED ABSTRACTIVE TEXT SUMMARIZATION: FINE-TUNING BART ON CNN/DAILYMAIL WITH BEAM SEARCH DECODING AND MULTI-METRIC EVALUATION</data>
  <data key="d6">TRANSFORMER-BASED ABSTRACTIVE TEXT SUMMARIZATION: FINE-TUNING BART ON CNN/DAILYMAIL WITH BEAM SEARCH DECODING AND MULTI-METRIC EVALUATION</data>
  <data key="d5">2025</data>
  <data key="d7">https://doi.org/10.12732/ijam.v38i12s.2113</data>
  <data key="d8">0</data>
  <data key="d9">The exponential growth of digital information demands scalable, accurate, and context-aware automatic text summarization systems. This paper presents a comprehensive study of Transformer-based abstractive text summarization, employing the BART-large-cnn encoder–decoder architecture fine-tuned on the CNN/DailyMail benchmark dataset (v3.0.0). The proposed methodology integrates teacher-forced supervised fine-tuning, cross-entropy loss with ROUGE-aware optimization, and beam search decoding with no-repeat n-gram constraints. We evaluate the model using ROUGE-1, ROUGE-2, ROUGE-L, and BERTScore F1 metrics on 500 held-out test samples and compare performance against 14 state-of-the-art systems reported in the recent literature. The fine-tuned BART model achieves ROUGE-1 = 0.748, ROUGE-2 = 0.691,</data>
</node>
<node id="author:spriha sinha">
  <data key="d0">Author</data>
  <data key="d1">Spriha Sinha</data>
</node>
<node id="paper:W4393669265">
  <data key="d0">Paper</data>
  <data key="d1">XSum and CNN with paraphrases of summaries</data>
  <data key="d6">XSum and CNN with paraphrases of summaries</data>
  <data key="d5">2023</data>
  <data key="d7">https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.10254975</data>
  <data key="d8">0</data>
  <data key="d9">These datasets contain the original datasets and paraphrases of the summaries. CNN/DailyMail is available at https://huggingface.co/datasets/cnn_dailymail released under the Apache 2.0 license. XSum is available at https://huggingface.co/datasets/EdinburghNLP/xsum and was originally released under the MIT license. The paraphraser is https://huggingface.co/humarin/chatgpt_paraphraser_on_T5_base available under the OpenRAIL license.</data>
</node>
<node id="paper:W2888556271">
  <data key="d0">Paper</data>
  <data key="d1">Neural Latent Extractive Document Summarization</data>
  <data key="d6">Neural Latent Extractive Document Summarization</data>
  <data key="d5">2018</data>
  <data key="d7">https://doi.org/10.18653/v1/d18-1088</data>
  <data key="d8">171</data>
  <data key="d9">Extractive summarization models require sentence-level labels, which are usually created heuristically (e.g., with rule-based methods) given that most summarization datasets only have document-summary pairs.Since these labels might be suboptimal, we propose a latent variable extractive model where sentences are viewed as latent variables and sentences with activated variables are used to infer gold summaries.During training the loss comes directly from gold summaries.Experiments on the CNN/Dailymail dataset show that our model improves over a strong extractive baseline trained on heuristically approximated labels and also performs competitively to several recent models.</data>
</node>
<node id="author:xingxing zhang">
  <data key="d0">Author</data>
  <data key="d1">Xingxing Zhang</data>
</node>
<node id="paper:W2962785754">
  <data key="d0">Paper</data>
  <data key="d1">HIBERT: Document Level Pre-training of Hierarchical Bidirectional Transformers for Document Summarization</data>
  <data key="d6">HIBERT: Document Level Pre-training of Hierarchical Bidirectional Transformers for Document Summarization</data>
  <data key="d5">2019</data>
  <data key="d7">https://doi.org/10.18653/v1/p19-1499</data>
  <data key="d8">389</data>
  <data key="d9">Neural extractive summarization models usually employ a hierarchical encoder for document encoding and they are trained using sentence-level labels, which are created heuristically using rule-based methods.Training the hierarchical encoder with these inaccurate labels is challenging.Inspired by the recent work on pre-training transformer sentence encoders (Devlin et al., 2018), we propose HIBERT (as shorthand for HIerachical Bidirectional Encoder Representations from Transformers) for document encoding and a method to pre-train it using unlabeled data.We apply the pre-trained HIBERT to our summarization model and it outperforms its randomly initialized counterpart by 1.25 ROUGE on the CNN/Dailymail dataset and by 2.0 ROUGE on a version of New York Times dataset.We also achieve the state-of</data>
</node>
<node id="paper:W2964028111">
  <data key="d0">Paper</data>
  <data key="d1">Searching for Effective Neural Extractive Summarization: What Works and What’s Next</data>
  <data key="d6">Searching for Effective Neural Extractive Summarization: What Works and What’s Next</data>
  <data key="d5">2019</data>
  <data key="d7">https://doi.org/10.18653/v1/p19-1100</data>
  <data key="d8">170</data>
  <data key="d9">The recent years have seen remarkable success in the use of deep neural networks on text summarization.However, there is no clear understanding of why they perform so well, or how they might be improved.In this paper, we seek to better understand how neural extractive summarization systems could benefit from different types of model architectures, transferable knowledge and learning schemas.Additionally, we find an effective way to improve current frameworks and achieve the state-ofthe-art result on CNN/DailyMail by a large margin based on our observations and analyses.Hopefully, our work could provide more clues for future research on extractive summarization.Source code will be available on Github 1 .</data>
</node>
<node id="author:ming zhong">
  <data key="d0">Author</data>
  <data key="d1">Ming Zhong</data>
</node>
<node id="author:pengfei liu">
  <data key="d0">Author</data>
  <data key="d1">Pengfei Liu</data>
</node>
