</node>
<node id="paper:W7212150204">
  <data key="d0">Paper</data>
  <data key="d1">IWSLT14 German-English, WMT14 English-German, and WMT16 English-Romanian</data>
  <data key="d6">IWSLT14 German-English, WMT14 English-German, and WMT16 English-Romanian</data>
  <data key="d5">2025</data>
  <data key="d7">https://doi.org/10.57760/sciencedb.j00104.00151</data>
  <data key="d8">0</data>
  <data key="d9">We conducted experiments on three widely used machine translation benchmarks: IWSLT14 German-English (De-En, ~160K sentence pairs), WMT14 English-German (En-De, ~4.5M sentence pairs), and WMT16 English-Romanian (En-Ro, ~610K sentence pairs). All datasets follow the standard splits of the respective benchmarks, and subword vocabularies are constructed using Byte Pair Encoding (BPE) to alleviate vocabulary sparsity and unify token granularity.</data>
</node>
