<node id="paper:W3174864715">
  <data key="d0">Paper</data>
  <data key="d1">R-Drop: Regularized Dropout for Neural Networks</data>
  <data key="d6">R-Drop: Regularized Dropout for Neural Networks</data>
  <data key="d5">2021</data>
  <data key="d7">https://doi.org/10.48550/arxiv.2106.14448</data>
  <data key="d8">343</data>
  <data key="d9">Dropout is a powerful and widely used technique to regularize the training of deep neural networks. In this paper, we introduce a simple regularization strategy upon dropout in model training, namely R-Drop, which forces the output distributions of different sub models generated by dropout to be consistent with each other. Specifically, for each training sample, R-Drop minimizes the bidirectional KL-divergence between the output distributions of two sub models sampled by dropout. Theoretical analysis reveals that R-Drop reduces the freedom of the model parameters and complements dropout. Experiments on $\bf{5}$ widely used deep learning tasks ($\bf{18}$ datasets in total), including neural machine translation, abstractive summarization, language understanding, language modeling, and image </data>
</node>
