<node id="paper:W7222651602">
  <data key="d0">Paper</data>
  <data key="d1">Verdict: Encode Once, Decide Many</data>
  <data key="d6">Verdict: Encode Once, Decide Many</data>
  <data key="d5">2026</data>
  <data key="d7">https://doi.org/10.48550/arxiv.2610.20778</data>
  <data key="d8">0</data>
  <data key="d9">Automating organizational work means answering many small typed questions about one piece of state (does this email need a reply, how urgent is it, who owns it) with probabilities good enough to act on without a human. The open typed-decision model we compare against, like a standard cross-encoder, answers each question with a separate pass over the input. We present Verdict-MQ, an open encoder that reads a case once and answers all of its questions: given identical retained state tokens after truncation (verified on every reported case), a structured attention mask makes the state encoding independent of the questions, keeps questions from influencing each other, and makes option order irrelevant by construction. On the typed-decisions benchmark, a 395M Verdict-MQ matches the soft gold la</data>
</node>
