  <data key="d0">Paper</data>
  <data key="d1">Inference Efficiency of CodeLlama and StarCoder on Self-Invoking HumanEval Pro Versus Original HumanEval Benchmarks</data>
  <data key="d6">Inference Efficiency of CodeLlama and StarCoder on Self-Invoking HumanEval Pro Versus Original HumanEval Benchmarks</data>
  <data key="d5">2026</data>
  <data key="d7">https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.20717619</data>
  <data key="d8">0</data>
  <data key="d9">We introduce self-invoking code generation, a new task designed to evaluate the progressive reasoning and problem-solving capabilities of LLMs. In this task, models are presented with a base problem and a related, more complex problem. They must solve the base problem and then utilize its solution to address the more complex one. This work features three key contributions. First, we propose a general recipe for generating more challenging versions of existing benchmarks, resulting in three new benchmarks: HumanEval Pro, MBPP Pro, and BigCodeBench-Lite Pro, specifically designed to assess LLMs Research goal: How does the inference efficiency (measured in tokens per second) of CodeLlama and StarCoder vary when solving self-invoking code generation tasks on HumanEval Pro compared to their eff</data>
</node>
