<node id="paper:W2962775040">
  <data key="d0">Paper</data>
  <data key="d1">Augmenting Librispeech with French Translations: A Multimodal Corpus for Direct Speech Translation Evaluation</data>
  <data key="d6">Augmenting Librispeech with French Translations: A Multimodal Corpus for Direct Speech Translation Evaluation</data>
  <data key="d5">2018</data>
  <data key="d7">https://doi.org/10.63317/4tgpdx9xax4j</data>
  <data key="d8">102</data>
  <data key="d9">Recent works in spoken language translation (SLT) have attempted to build end-to-end speech-to-text translation without using source language transcription during learning or decoding.However, while large quantities of parallel texts (such as Europarl, OpenSubtitles) are available for training machine translation systems, there are no large (&gt;100h) and open source parallel corpora that include speech in a source language aligned to text in a target language.This paper tries to fill this gap by augmenting an existing (monolingual) corpus: LibriSpeech.This corpus, used for automatic speech recognition, is derived from read audiobooks from the LibriVox project, and has been carefully segmented and aligned.After gathering French e-books corresponding to the English audio-books from LibriSpeech</data>
</node>
<node id="paper:W3015337526">
  <data key="d0">Paper</data>
  <data key="d1">Detection and Analysis of T/D Deletion in Librispeech</data>
  <data key="d6">Detection and Analysis of T/D Deletion in Librispeech</data>
  <data key="d5">2020</data>
  <data key="d7">https://doi.org/10.1109/icassp40776.2020.9053121</data>
  <data key="d8">0</data>
  <data key="d9">In this study we developed a new method for automatic identification of t/d deletion. Our method achieved 94% accuracy on TIMIT and 87% on human-annotated data from Librispeech. We then conducted an analysis of t/d deletion on more than 500k tokens in Librispeech. The following results were found: (1) /d/ is more likely to be deleted than /t/; (2) t/d is more likely to be deleted when preceded by a nasal or a coronal obstruent; (3) In terms of the following phone, the rate of t/d deletion from low to high was: vowels and pausesemi-weak past tense &gt; regular past tense; (5) t/d is less likely to be deleted when the phonological neighborhood density (PND) is higher.</data>
</node>
