<node id="paper:W2029199293">
  <data key="d0">Paper</data>
  <data key="d1">TCD-TIMIT: An Audio-Visual Corpus of Continuous Speech</data>
  <data key="d6">TCD-TIMIT: An Audio-Visual Corpus of Continuous Speech</data>
  <data key="d5">2015</data>
  <data key="d7">https://doi.org/10.1109/tmm.2015.2407694</data>
  <data key="d8">251</data>
  <data key="d9">Automatic audio-visual speech recognition currently lags behind its audio-only counterpart in terms of major progress. One of the reasons commonly cited by researchers is the scarcity of suitable research corpora. This paper details the creation of a new corpus designed for continuous audio-visual speech recognition research . TCD-TIMIT consists of high-quality audio and video footage of 62 speakers reading a total of 6913 phonetically rich sentences. Three of the speakers are professionally-trained lipspeakers, recorded to test the hypothesis that lipspeakers may have an advantage over regular speakers in automatic visual speech recognition systems. Video footage was recorded from two angles: straight on, and at 30°. The paper outlines the recording of footage, and the required post-proce</data>
</node>
