<node id="paper:W4210423654">
  <data key="d0">Paper</data>
  <data key="d1">Decoupling Recognition and Transcription in Mandarin ASR</data>
  <data key="d6">Decoupling Recognition and Transcription in Mandarin ASR</data>
  <data key="d5">2021</data>
  <data key="d7">https://doi.org/10.1109/asru51503.2021.9688053</data>
  <data key="d8">13</data>
  <data key="d9">Much of the recent literature on automatic speech recognition (ASR) is taking an end-to-end approach. Unlike English where the writing system is closely related to sound, Chinese characters (Hanzi) represent meaning, not sound. We propose factoring audio → Hanzi into two sub-tasks: (1) audio → Pinyin and (2) Pinyin → Hanzi, where Pinyin is a system of phonetic transcription of standard Chinese. Factoring the audio → Hanzi task in this way achieves 3.9% CER (character error rate) on the Aishell-1 corpus, the best result reported on this dataset so far.</data>
</node>
