  <data key="d9">Cora Histórico Digital is a reproducible historical-digital dataset and computational corpus derived from the Cora–Spanish vocabulary associated with José de Ortega. The project preserves the historical witness, provenance, OCR evidence, computational transformations and uncertainty states required to audit the construction of the corpus. The historical work was first printed in Mexico in 1732. This release uses as its working witness the 1888 Tepic reprint, Vocabulario de las lenguas castellana y cora, preserved digitally by the John Carter Brown Library and Internet Archive (vocabulariodelas00orte). Version 0.1.0 contains 2,140 computational candidates from the alphabetical vocabulary: 2,137 are classified as machine_accepted, 2 as machine_rejected, and 1 as machine_uncertain. The datase</data>
