  <data key="d8">0</data>
  <data key="d9">Péter Jacsó reviews CiteSeer in the November issue of his Digital Reference Shelf.</data>
</node>
<node id="paper:W4409710037">
  <data key="d0">Paper</data>
  <data key="d1">CiteSeer is OAI-compliant</data>
  <data key="d6">CiteSeer is OAI-compliant</data>
  <data key="d5">2004</data>
  <data key="d7">https://doi.org/10.63485/3qxnz-d8d66</data>
  <data key="d8">0</data>
  <data key="d9">CiteSeer is now OAI-compliant. The announcement page has technical details for harvesters and those who want to download CiteSeers's OAI records. (PS: This is an important development. CiteSeer was already OA, and already had the eyeballs of computer scientists worldwide.</data>
</node>
<node id="paper:W7128778099">
  <data key="d0">Paper</data>
  <data key="d1">CiteSeer Co-Citation</data>
  <data key="d6">CiteSeer Co-Citation</data>
  <data key="d5">2026</data>
  <data key="d7">https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.18632376</data>
  <data key="d8">0</data>
  <data key="d9">The CiteSeer co-citation network is a hypergraph where nodes are scientific papers from the CiteSeer dataset and hyperedges are sets of papers that are co-cited together by another paper. Each node is labeled with the paper's research field, chosen from six computer science topics: Agents, Artificial Intelligence (AI), Databases (DB), Information Retrieval (IR), Machine Learning (ML), and Human-Computer Interaction (HCI). This dataset is commonly used as a benchmark in hypergraph learning tasks. Find the dataset details in AHORN.</data>
</node>
