<node id="paper:W7163057080">
  <data key="d0">Paper</data>
  <data key="d1">Multi-Scale Contrastive Graph Augmentation Strategies and GNN Inference Efficiency on Large-Scale Datasets</data>
  <data key="d6">Multi-Scale Contrastive Graph Augmentation Strategies and GNN Inference Efficiency on Large-Scale Datasets</data>
  <data key="d5">2026</data>
  <data key="d7">https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.20482801</data>
  <data key="d8">0</data>
  <data key="d9">This report synthesises findings from 15 peer-reviewed papers addressing the following research question: How do different graph augmentation strategies in multi-scale contrastive learning affect the efficiency of GNN inference on large-scale datasets like OGBN-arXiv, as measured by throughput and memory. We trained a large, deep convolutional neural network to classify the 1.2 million high-resolution images in the ImageNet LSVRC-2010 contest into the 1000 different classes. On the test data, we achieved top-1 and top-5 error rates of 37.5\% and 17.0\%, respectively, which is. 5 claims were extracted from source literature; 5 were independently verified against retrieved documents. An automated multi-reviewer quality assessment produced a score of 9.5/10. This report is a machine-generated</data>
</node>
<node id="paper:W7164416899">
  <data key="d0">Paper</data>
  <data key="d1">Quantum–AI for High-Dimensional Learning: Graph Neural Networks, Topological Data Analysis, Tensor Networks, and Sparse Predictive Modelling</data>
  <data key="d6">Quantum–AI for High-Dimensional Learning: Graph Neural Networks, Topological Data Analysis, Tensor Networks, and Sparse Predictive Modelling</data>
  <data key="d5">2026</data>
  <data key="d7">https://doi.org/10.62311/nesx/rpj2-30062026</data>
  <data key="d8">0</data>
  <data key="d9">Abstract: This study investigates a unified Quantum-AI framework for high-dimensional learning in settings where data are simultaneously relational, geometric, tensorial, and sparse. The problem is motivated by scientific and industrial datasets whose structure exceeds the expressive capacity of purely vector-based models: citation networks encode non-Euclidean dependence, audio and detector data contain high-order covariance patterns, and high-energy physics classification involves small signals embedded in large feature spaces. The proposed framework combines graph neural networks for relational propagation, topological data analysis for filtration-stable shape descriptors, tensor networks for low-rank multilinear compression, and sparse predictive modelling for interpretable feature sel</data>
</node>
<node id="author:murali krishna pasupuleti">
  <data key="d0">Author</data>
  <data key="d1">Murali Krishna Pasupuleti</data>
</node>
<node id="paper:W4417332592">
  <data key="d0">Paper</data>
  <data key="d1">Improved and domain specific model soups</data>
  <data key="d6">Improved and domain specific model soups</data>
  <data key="d5">2025</data>
  <data key="d7">https://doi.org/10.31274/td-20251215-195</data>
  <data key="d8">0</data>
  <data key="d9">This thesis investigates advanced model aggregation techniques, specifically Model Soups, to enhance the performance and efficiency of Graph Neural Networks (GNNs). Traditional ensemble methods, while accurate, incur significant computational and memory costs, limiting scalability. We propose two novel souping algorithms: Learned Souping (LS), which employs gradient descent to optimize weight interpolation for improved accuracy and speed, and Partition Learned Souping (PLS), a variant which reduces memory usage through graph partitioning. Evaluated on benchmark datasets (Flickr, Reddit, ogbn-arxiv, ogbn-products) and GNN architectures (GCN, GAT, GraphSAGE), LS achieves up to 1.2% accuracy improvement and 2.1X speedup, while PLS delivers a 24.5X speedup and 76% memory reduction without comp</data>
</node>
