<node id="paper:W4404570455">
  <data key="d0">Paper</data>
  <data key="d1">STAMP: Symmetric Temporal Averaging Message Passing for Domain Adaptation in Chronologically Split Graphs</data>
  <data key="d6">STAMP: Symmetric Temporal Averaging Message Passing for Domain Adaptation in Chronologically Split Graphs</data>
  <data key="d5">2024</data>
  <data key="d7">https://doi.org/10.48550/arxiv.2411.10957</data>
  <data key="d8">0</data>
  <data key="d9">This paper addresses domain adaptation in graph-structured data under chronological splits. We consider transductive semi-supervised node classification (SSNC), in which labels of historical nodes are used to predict labels of more recent nodes. Temporal dependencies in graph connectivity induce distribution shifts across time, often degrading the performance of models trained on past nodes. Despite its practical relevance, domain adaptation under chronological graph splits remains relatively underexplored. We propose Symmetric Temporal Averaging Message Passing (STAMP), a timestamp-balanced operator that replaces standard neighborhood aggregation with temporally aligned propagation. We call its specialized variant for the heterogeneous ogbn-mag dataset STAMP-mag, reserving STAMP for the g</data>
</node>
