<node id="paper:W7204552310">
  <data key="d0">Paper</data>
  <data key="d1">QFC Pre-Registration — Chest Radiology Domain: ChestX-ray14 (NIH Clinical Center, PA→AP Protocol Shift and Resolution Shift)</data>
  <data key="d6">QFC Pre-Registration — Chest Radiology Domain: ChestX-ray14 (NIH Clinical Center, PA→AP Protocol Shift and Resolution Shift)</data>
  <data key="d5">2026</data>
  <data key="d7">https://doi.org/10.17605/osf.io/t6hvm</data>
  <data key="d8">0</data>
  <data key="d9">This pre-registration specifies the complete analysis plan for the chest radiology domain of the QFC multi-domain universality study. The dataset is NIH ChestX-ray14 (Wang et al. 2017, public domain, NIH Clinical Center) — 112,120 frontal chest X-rays from 30,805 unique patients with 14 pathology labels. This domain is part of Category 8 (Medical Images) in the study's 18-category empirical framework covering all major categories of real-world AI deployment data. Scientific question: Does attribution instability emerge in chest X-ray classification AI under PA→AP protocol shift? Additionally: does the feature extraction approach determine whether collapse occurs — specifically, do generic invariant features suppress collapse that environment-specific features reveal? Prior experiments in t</data>
</node>
