<node id="paper:W7222884741">
  <data key="d0">Paper</data>
  <data key="d1">Zero-shot performance of the CheXzero and CLEAR chest radiograph foundation models by sex and age on an external dataset (NIH ChestX-ray14): a preregistered evaluation</data>
  <data key="d6">Zero-shot performance of the CheXzero and CLEAR chest radiograph foundation models by sex and age on an external dataset (NIH ChestX-ray14): a preregistered evaluation</data>
  <data key="d5">2026</data>
  <data key="d7">https://doi.org/10.17605/osf.io/dsbem</data>
  <data key="d8">0</data>
  <data key="d9">This study evaluates two openly released vision-language foundation models for chest radiographs, CheXzero and CLEAR, on the official test split of NIH ChestX-ray14. Neither model was trained on NIH images. Both are run zero-shot, with no change to their weights, on the 14 NIH findings. Performance is reported overall and separately by patient sex and age group. CheXzero has published subgroup audits and serves as a replication anchor for the pipeline. CLEAR, published in Nature Biomedical Engineering in 2026, has no published subgroup evaluation; its own external evaluation used VinDr-CXR, PadChest, the Indiana University collection and the CheXpert test set, and did not report results by sex or age. The study is part of an M.Tech dissertation at Mewar University and follows a systematic </data>
</node>
