<node id="paper:W7204705310">
  <data key="d0">Paper</data>
  <data key="d1">WSI feature, BRACS, CPTAC, CAMELYON16, CAMELYON17</data>
  <data key="d6">WSI feature, BRACS, CPTAC, CAMELYON16, CAMELYON17</data>
  <data key="d5">2026</data>
  <data key="d7">https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.22166569</data>
  <data key="d8">0</data>
  <data key="d9">This dataset contains pre-extracted whole-slide image (WSI) features for four pathology benchmarks: CAMELYON16 (C16), CAMELYON17 (C17), BRACS, and CPTAC. Features are extracted from image patches using two different encoders: (1) a ResNet-18 pretrained on ImageNet, and (2) a self-supervised Vision Transformer (ViT) pretrained on histopathology whole-slide images. These features are provided to facilitate reproducible evaluation of multiple-instance learning (MIL) methods without requiring users to repeat computationally expensive WSI preprocessing and feature extraction.</data>
</node>
<node id="author:linfeng ye">
  <data key="d0">Author</data>
  <data key="d1">Linfeng Ye</data>
</node>
<node id="paper:W4362694454">
  <data key="d0">Paper</data>
  <data key="d1">Background detection affects downstream classification of Camelyon16 whole slide images</data>
  <data key="d6">Background detection affects downstream classification of Camelyon16 whole slide images</data>
  <data key="d5">2023</data>
  <data key="d7">https://doi.org/10.1117/12.2653882</data>
  <data key="d8">2</data>
  <data key="d9">Detecting the background is often one of the first tasks when analyzing whole slide images. However, these methods greatly vary and are generally not reported in sufficient detail for subsequent studies to be able to reproduce. Therefore, we sought to determine the effect of varying background detection methods on downstream performance metrics. Specifically, we applied attention-based multiple instance learning, CLAM, and Attention2majority to classify whole slide images from Camelyon16 using four different background detection methods. Our results show that performance metrics (i.e., accuracy and area under the curve - AUC) differ when different background detection methods are utilized. Furthermore, all classification methods perform worse when using hand-drawn tissue annotations for ba</data>
</node>
<node id="author:thomas erol tavolara">
  <data key="d0">Author</data>
  <data key="d1">Thomas Erol Tavolara</data>
</node>
