<node id="paper:W7221547511">
  <data key="d0">Paper</data>
  <data key="d1">Is It the Encoder or the Head? Slide-Level Robustness of Pathology Foundation Models to Acquisition Artifacts on CAMELYON16</data>
  <data key="d6">Is It the Encoder or the Head? Slide-Level Robustness of Pathology Foundation Models to Acquisition Artifacts on CAMELYON16</data>
  <data key="d5">2026</data>
  <data key="d7">https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.23323557</data>
  <data key="d8">0</data>
  <data key="d9">A previous report found that much of the accuracy a frozen pathology foundation model loses under image degradation belongs to the linear probe on top, not to the encoder. That study used patch classification. Here we ask the same question for slide-level detection of lymph-node metastases on CAMELYON16 (399 whole-slide images, 871,867 tiles at about 10x), with four frozen encoders, seven test conditions and five seeds. With an attention-based multiple-instance head, training on a mix of clean and degraded tile embeddings recovers much of the loss to JPEG compression for Phikon and Kaiko B/16: at quality 10, which the head never saw, slide AUC rises from 63.1 to 79.7 and from 67.9 to 81.1, with no change on clean slides. It does not help Phikon-v2. Three analyses show the tumour signal sur</data>
</node>
